From b928d7adc1e438b1f5d835e19173cc24ead24a7c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HuskerMinion <28571744+HuskerMinion@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 29/40] ALSA: arm: Add Realtek RTD1295 audio CPU playback
 driver

The RTD129x audio outputs are driven by the audio CPU firmware. Add a
single playback PCM that follows the QNAP/Realtek vendor ALSA driver
(snd-realtek.c without AO_ON_SCPU): the ALSA buffer is the input ring of
a firmware LPCM decoder, which feeds a "flash" input pin of the firmware
audio output through one ring per channel.

Setup and teardown are firmware RPCs (create AO and decoder, get/release
the flash pin, init rings, connect, pause/run/stop/flush/destroy, new
format in-band command). Streaming needs no RPC: an hrtimer hands the
application's data to the decoder by moving the ring write pointer and
reads the decoder's read pointer back as the position. Data handed to
the audio CPU cannot be taken back, so rewinds are not allowed
(SNDRV_PCM_INFO_NO_REWINDS). On drain the
decoder gets an EOS in-band command; on close the driver waits for the
AO to play what was decoded.

S16_LE stereo at 44.1/48 kHz only; alsa-lib converts the rest. All
shared memory comes from a no-map reserved pool below 512 MiB (the
audio CPU's KSEG1 window), instead of the vendor ION heap. An optional
GPIO enables the speaker amplifier while the PCM is open (or always,
with amp_always_on, as the vendor driver does), and there is a 0..31
master volume.

SND_ARM is allowed on ARM64 so that the driver can live with the other
ARM SoC drivers.
---
 sound/arm/Kconfig      |  14 +-
 sound/arm/Makefile     |   3 +
 sound/arm/rtd1295-ao.c | 940 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 956 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 create mode 100644 sound/arm/rtd1295-ao.c

